---
title: "Disentangling Attentional Shift and Attentional Competition Accounts of
  Associative Blocking"
subtitle: "Replication and Extension of Beesley & Le Pelley
  ([2011](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0019526))"
authors:
  - name: Josef Mana
    id: jm
    orcid: 0000-0002-7817-3978
    email: josef.mana@psu.cas.cz
    affiliation:
      - name: Institute of Psychology of the Czech Academy of Sciences
        department: Cognitive Psychology
        city: Prague
        address: Pod Vodárenskou věží 4, Prague 18200
        country: Czech Republic
    roles: [conceptualization, data curation]
    corresponding: true
abstract: >
  Forward blocking, the attenuated learning about a cue presented together
  with another cue that already predicts the outcome, is a cornerstone
  phenomenon of associative learning. In the three-stage design of
  @lepelley2007, the associative theories cannot explain it without an
  attentional mechanism, yet either of two such mechanisms, rapid attentional
  shifts or attentional competition, suffices to explain it, and no experiment
  has yet decided between them. @paskewitz2020 derived a design that could.
  Because blocking may be less reliable than its standing suggests, we first
  test whether the three-stage result of @beesley2011 replicates online.
  Experiment 1 is a pre-registered online replication in which participants
  complete a within-participant three-stage allergist task. Blocking is tested
  by one-sided one-sample *t*-tests of the original confidence-weighted index,
  deciding the replication, and of a choice-based index, selecting the dependent
  variable for the extension. The sample size follows from a power analysis
  informed by a pilot study, within a two-look group-sequential design whose
  second look adds participants only if neither index detects blocking at the
  first. Experiment 2 will implement the design of @paskewitz2020 to adjudicate
  between the attentional shift and attentional competition accounts and
  Experiment 3 will test its conclusions in a formally identical design
  with a different task.
keywords:
  - Attention
  - Learning
  - Cue competition
  - Blocking
bibliography: ../../common/references.bib
